**Microlyte (A2005)**

Microlyte is Imbed's FDA 510(k)-cleared antimicrobial wound dressing (K153756). It's an ultrathin synthetic matrix that conforms to the wound bed and carries ionic silver for sustained antimicrobial activity; the manufacturer's in vitro data shows 99.99% reduction held past 72 hours. It resorbs on its own in about a week, so there's no removal step, and there's no refrigeration or animal-derived material to deal with. The appeal is that you're managing bioburden and supporting healing with one product.

On the evidence, the chronic-wound study (Wounds 2020;32(11):309–318, "Efficacy of a Bioresorbable Matrix in Healing Complex Chronic Wounds") ran 32 patients across 35 wounds and reported 91% healed or significantly improved by 12 weeks with a 73% average area reduction. There's also an SSI-prevention case series (Chatelain, Wounds 2021;33(10):245–252) where prophylactic Microlyte SURGICAL in at-risk diabetic foot and ankle surgery saw no SSIs in the per-protocol group against a roughly 13% historical baseline. That one's a small single-arm series, so I'd read it before quoting it to a committee.

Coverage is the part worth understanding. It bills under A2005 per square centimeter, applied with CPT 15271–15278. NGS doesn't have an LCD for skin substitutes, so coverage falls to Section 1862(a)(1)(A) of the Social Security Act, the general "reasonable and necessary" standard. There's no mandated four-week failed-conservative-care window and no application cap, which is what lets you use it early instead of waiting. The catch is that without an LCD it's a case-by-case call, so the note has to carry it: name the contamination or bioburden risk, say Microlyte was applied to reduce that bioburden through its silver activity, give your reasoning for choosing an antimicrobial matrix over a plain dressing, and confirm the application matched the cleared indication and the IFU. The 510(k) and IFU are in the packet so the note can point to them.

**Interfyl vs. Xcellistem**

I've attached the new Interfyl sizing guidance along with the Xcellistem 510(k) and its studies. The quick version is that they bill the same way (both per-1-mg codes, both landing at $50 a billable unit), but they're designed to do almost opposite things in the inflammatory phase. Xcellistem dissolves and provokes; Interfyl persists and settles things down.

| | Xcellistem (A2004) | Interfyl (Q4171) |
|---|---|---|
| Source | Porcine lung + spleen (xenograft) | Human placental chorionic plate (allograft) |
| Regulatory | 510(k) device, K172593, product code KGN | 361 HCT/P (21 CFR 1271), no FDA clearance |
| Form | Dry powder: 250 / 500 / 1000 mg | Flowable gel 0.3–1.5 mL; particulate 50 / 100 mg |
| Maker | StemSys / FetTech (RTT, Bobber) | Celularity, distributed by Arthrex |
| Billing | per 1 mg | per 1 mg |

The core difference is degradation. Xcellistem is built to break down fast; the powder disintegrates and releases matricryptic peptides, GAGs, and growth factors into the bed all at once. Interfyl is meant to stay put and fill a soft-tissue deficit, remodeling slowly as host cells move in. That's where the infected-wound argument comes from. Kimmel's poster makes the point that since the matrix is supposed to disappear anyway, bacterial protease breakdown isn't a failure mode, so you can put it on a colonized bed right away. Interfyl can't make that claim; a scaffold whose whole job is to persist in dead space is the last thing you want to implant into infection.

They also aim in opposite directions on immune response. Porcine ECM works partly by driving inflammation, with brisk macrophage recruitment followed by the M1-to-M2 shift toward remodeling; Sasse's two-week biopsy showed exactly that, with neovascularization and no foreign-body granulomas. Interfyl sells the reverse. It's decellularized, not cross-linked, and human, so the pitch is a quieter immune and inflammatory response, and the in vitro work backs it up: Mao et al. found Interfyl lowered pro-inflammatory cytokine, growth-factor, and MMP expression while raising TGF-β3 against competing placental matrices. TGF-β3 over TGF-β1 is the scar-free-healing axis, so there's real mechanism there, not just a slogan. In practice, Xcellistem is trying to restart a stalled wound and Interfyl is trying to rebuild soft tissue without kicking off fibrosis.

On composition, Xcellistem's selling point is a deliberately low collagen fraction, around 45% collagen with 25% elastin, 14% fibronectin, and 7% laminin, versus roughly 90% collagen for single-tissue ECMs, so you get more integrin-binding variety per milligram. That's why they went with lung and spleen. Interfyl has the same general ingredients but leans on scaffold quality and immune quietness instead of a growth-factor payload. One thing to be careful with: those Xcellistem percentages come off a manufacturer poster, not independent testing, so don't use them with customers as if they'd been third-party verified.

The cleanest real difference is indication. Xcellistem has cleared Indications for Use that name DFU, VLU, pressure ulcers, and burns. Interfyl is a 361 HCT/P, so it has no cleared indication at all and can only use homologous-use language ("replace or supplement damaged or inadequate integumental tissue," "fill irregular spaces or soft-tissue deficits"). A 361 product legally can't claim to treat a disease. If you're ever putting the two side by side, that's the strongest honest edge Xcellistem has.

